 ## Background
-The shift from software-based ray casting to hardware-accelerated implementations was driven by the need for real-time interaction with large volumetric datasets. Early implementations leveraged the fixed-function texture hardware of graphics cards to perform trilinear interpolation, which was significantly faster than CPU-based interpolation [5616-hardware-accelerated-volume-rendering.md]. As programmable shaders became available, more complex optical models, lighting, and acceleration techniques like early ray termination could be integrated directly into the rendering pipeline [7443-volume-ray-casting-on-graphics-hardware.md]. Modern implementations continue to evolve, supporting complex data structures like AMR and unstructured meshes, as well as advanced features like path tracing in web environments [8592-webgl-path-tracing-volume-rendering.md][10503-hardware-accelerated-amr-volume-rendering-via-homogenization.md].
+The shift from CPU-based ray casting to GPU implementations began with the advent of programmable graphics pipelines (e.g., DirectX 9, OpenGL Shading Language). Early implementations utilized multi-pass techniques to manage state and leverage early-z testing for early ray termination [7443-volume-ray-casting-on-graphics-hardware.md]. Subsequent developments focused on single-pass fragment shader implementations for real-time performance [5616-hardware-accelerated-volume-rendering.md], support for complex data structures like AMR [10503-hardware-accelerated-amr-volume-rendering-via-homogenization.md] and unstructured meshes [9631-gpu-based-tiled-ray-casting-with-depth-peeling.md], and web-based rendering via WebGL [8592-webgl-path-tracing-volume-rendering.md].
 
